### 9a6086f
fix(state): raise the native TypeError for push and pop on a nullish receiver

The direct wrappers read the receiver's [HOOKS] slot before anything
else, so push.call(null) failed on that read with a message about the
internal symbol, where the five Proxy-receiver wrappers let the native
method raise its own TypeError. Read the slot with optional chaining so
a nullish receiver reads nothing and falls through to the native call.
The test pins the message to what the native method raises on the
running Node, since V8 has worded it differently across versions.
Measured against the parent commit over three interleaved runs of the
three array cases: hookless 22.81ms against 22.60, hooks 24.51 against
24.53, undefined 22.98 against 22.86, inside the run-to-run spread.

### 350b48f
perf(state): load the raw value once before the internal-symbol check

The get traps read the raw target at two sites: one on the early return
for the internal symbols, one for every other property. The direct push
and pop of ticket 07 read [HOOKS] through the first site with array
receivers only, so its feedback held nothing but array maps. Those maps
die with the arrays between benchmark samples, the site reports no
feedback, and V8 deoptimizes the whole trap on the next direct call:
twice per benchmark cycle, where the base trap is never deoptimized
after warm-up. The recompiled trap read a plain property 4% slower.

Load the value first and return it for an internal symbol, so every read
shares the one megamorphic site whose feedback never dies. Move 4: the
symbol read's feedback leaves the hot trap's deoptimization path.
Measured over three interleaved triplets, cycles 2 and 3: state read
(no effect) 16.63ms against 16.84 base and 17.16 on 92f1922; the hookless
trap shows no deoptimization after warm-up with --trace-deopt.

### 92f1922
perf(state): check existence only for an equal undefined write

Object.hasOwn on the equal branch cost 35% on a write of an equal value
(12.7ms to 17.2ms per million). A read that is not undefined already
proves the property exists on the target or its prototype, so only an
equal undefined write needs the check, and `prop in rawTarget` does it
for 4% on that write alone. Move 1: do less work per operation.

