### 91a93af
perf(state): skip the proxyCache entry for a target that carries its Proxy

The base commit's creation profile puts 65% of the time in the garbage
collector. Every state() call inserted its target into the proxyCache
WeakMap, and ephemeron tables cost the collector per entry, yet for an
extensible target the entry is never consulted: state() returns the
[PROXY] symbol first, and the nested wrap can read the same symbol.

Insert into proxyCache only when the target could not take the symbol,
which defineProxyProperties now reports, and let wrapNestedObject read
[PROXY] before falling back to state(). Measured per 100k calls in a
targeted script: object creation 25.4ms to 9.8ms, array creation 25.1ms
to 18.8ms (below the base commit despite the HOOKS define), nested read
36.6ms to 33.1ms per million. Moves 1 and 4: less work per state(),
and no bookkeeping where nobody reads it.

### 3a8e13a
perf(state): carry the direct array context in the [HOOKS] slot

Profiling state creation of 100k arrays showed 2.7x the time of the
base commit (27ms to 74ms), 76% of it in the garbage collector: the
WeakMap keyed by the Proxy that held each array's direct context adds
an ephemeron entry per array. Object creation was unchanged.

Store the context on the array target under the HOOKS symbol instead,
which an extensible hooked target already defines and nothing reads.
The shared push and pop wrappers read it through their Proxy receiver,
where the get trap's internal-symbol guard returns the raw slot, so no
read-path code changes. The context is a class so the wrappers tell it
from the hooks object a non-array receiver keeps in the same slot, as
push.call(objectState) does; a test pins that receiver. Array creation
drops to 40ms with this change alone (moves 1 and 4: no second WeakMap
and no ephemeron work).

### 1c1191f
fix(state): store a write of undefined to an absent property

The set trap compared the new value with rawTarget[prop], which reads
undefined for an absent property, so a write of undefined to a property
that did not exist was dropped: no own element, no wake, no onWrite, and
a growing index write did not even move length. A plain array stores it.
Trunk had this on hookless state; tickets 02 to 06 spread it to hooked
state. Principle 1: changing a state changes the state and notifies.

Apply the equal skip only to a property that exists, with Object.hasOwn
on the equal branch alone, in the shared set trap for every write path.
The direct push treats each new index as a change, since it grew the
array; its length write stays equal because the index writes already
grew the raw length, as the Proxy path sees it. Pin both paths and the
plain Proxy reference, plus pop of an own undefined (Q65 a).

